ctually  happens inside the cell know how big is  a mammalian cell as you will see sizes  of cells in an organism vary  considerably at one extreme we have very  tiny cells like endothelial cells red  blood cells these are very very small  but the other end we have like the  reproductive cells like the egg which is  100 to 200 microns in diameter  whatever the cells that can stretch four  centimeters like the muscle cells and  certain nerve cells now in this problem  we're going to be dealing with the red  blood cells one of the most abundant  cells in the body as you can see in this  very colorful picture a red blood cell  can be approximated by a cylinder six to  eight microns in diameter right so  knowing that we can actually calculate  some dimensions of the red blood cells  such as the total volume and their  surface area  now here is a cylinder by which we  approximate a red blood cell and let's  say this cylinder has the height H and  the radius R from our problem we know H  is about two microns and R well we know  the diameter it's like six to eight  microns so R is gonna be let's go is the  middle 3.5 microns now as you remember  from from geometry then the volume of  the cylinder is gonna be PI R squared H  which when we substitute our units we're  gonna get about 77 cubic microns all  right we'll come back to discussing the  units in a little bit now the surface  area so it's gonna be the area  surrounding the cylinder plus the two  circles on top in the bottom so the two  circles are two PI R squared plus the  surrounding area it's gonna be 2 PI R H  and that comes out to be about a hundred  twenty four ninety five square microns  now how much is really one cubic micron  let's try to relate it to a unit that  we're more familiar such as leader right  so well one milliliter is actually one  cubic centimeter right one microliter is  one cubic millimeter  now one cubic micrometer it's like a  billion times smaller than one  microliter one way because it's a